---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';
import MermaidDiagram from '@/components/MermaidDiagram.astro';
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' }, { label: 'Hooks and A2A' }]}>
  <h1>Hooks and agent-to-agent communication</h1>
  <p>Two hook mechanisms and one A2A relay participate in the runtime. Each has its own sender, authentication, and state.</p>

  <h2 id="coder-hooks">Coder lifecycle events</h2>
  <MermaidDiagram title="Coder event relay through Manager" source={`sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Coder
    participant M as Manager hook ingress
    participant N as Nexus hook server
    participant R as Tenant Redis
    C->>M: Signed lifecycle event
    M->>M: Verify signature and tenant mapping
    M->>N: Relay body with tenant signature
    N->>N: Verify tenant signature
    N->>R: Record audit event
    N-->>M: Hook decision
    M-->>C: Hook decision`} />
  <ol>
    <li>Coder sends a signed lifecycle event to Manager's <code>/api/v1/hooks/coder/chat</code> endpoint.</li>
    <li>Manager verifies the global signature and payload hash, resolves the Coder workspace to exactly one tenant, then signs the same body with that tenant's hook key.</li>
    <li>Manager relays the event to the Nexus hook server at <code>/experimental/hooks/chat</code>. Nexus verifies the tenant signature, writes an audit event to tenant Redis, and may wake its flow loop.</li>
    <li>Nexus returns a hook decision through Manager to the Coder sender.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="agent-hooks">Agent CLI hooks</h2>
  <p>Nexus publishes role-specific shell hooks and agent assets to a shared artifacts volume. A Forge or Sentinel workspace copies its own hooks during startup and configures its local agent client. These local scripts call the harness; they are distinct from Coder's HTTP lifecycle events.</p>

  <h2 id="a2a">Forge and Sentinel A2A verification</h2>
  <MermaidDiagram title="Forge and Sentinel exchange evidence through Nexus" source={`sequenceDiagram
    participant S as Sentinel
    participant N as Nexus A2A relay
    participant R as Tenant Redis
    participant F as Forge
    S->>N: Verification task with pair token
    N->>R: Store task and token-bound state
    F->>N: Claim authorized task
    F->>F: Run verification in Forge workspace
    F->>N: Send progress and result
    N->>R: Persist result and audit evidence
    S->>N: Read result
    N-->>S: Verification evidence`} />
  <p>Nexus derives a ticket pair token and stores its hash in tenant Redis. Manager passes the raw token when it creates the paired worker workspaces. Sentinel sends a verification request to the Nexus JSON-RPC relay. Forge claims the task, runs the allowed verification in its own workspace, and sends progress and results back. Sentinel reads the recorded result without entering Forge's filesystem.</p>
  <p>The relay stores tasks, results, and audit evidence in tenant Redis so it can recover after a Nexus restart. Manager provisions and scopes the participants, but Forge and Sentinel send A2A traffic directly to Nexus.</p>
  <Callout type="info" title="Different tokens">Coder hook JWTs authenticate lifecycle delivery. A2A pair tokens authenticate one verification pair. Runtime access tokens authenticate workspace calls to Manager. None substitutes for another.</Callout>
</DocsLayout>
